// Palace (Shithead) — stress-test implementation, DSL only, no `instantiate`.
//
// Source: https://www.pagat.com/beating/shithead.html (McLeod's rules, the
// project's authoritative reference). 4 players. Each is dealt 3 face-down
// "blind" cards, 3 face-up cards on top of them, and a 3-card hand; the rest
// of the deck is the stock. Before play, a player may swap any number of
// hand cards with their own face-up cards. Play goes clockwise onto a single
// shared pile: a play must equal or beat the pile's effective top rank.
// Twos are wild (playable on anything; anything is playable on a two — the
// pile "resets"). Tens always burn the pile and win the actor another turn.
// Four cards of matching rank in a row on top also burn and win another
// turn. A player who cannot play must pick up the whole pile. After playing
// from hand, a player redraws from stock up to 3 cards, stock permitting. A
// player's hand must be exhausted before their face-up cards, and face-up
// before their blind cards; blind cards are flipped unseen — the player
// never learns a blind card's identity until it lands face-up on the pile,
// at which point (if it doesn't beat the pile) the player must pick up the
// whole pile including that card. The last player still holding any cards
// (hand + face-up + blind) is the loser.
//
// Simplifications (every one is a deliberate, declared cut, not a silent
// drop):
//
//  1. Fixed at 4 players. Pagat allows 2-6 (6 needs two jokers added); the
//     grammar has a `players: <lo>..<hi>` range form, but no corpus game
//     exercises it and a variable-arity ring would ripple through every
//     zone/index in this file for no stress-test benefit. Documented, not
//     hidden.
//  2. Single-card plays only. The reference rules let a player lay down
//     several cards of matching rank in one play (e.g. both black sevens
//     together). Modelling a *multi-card* play as one move needs the
//     movement grammar to move a chosen-size *set* selected by a joint
//     predicate over combinations, which the corpus's `chosen N cards`
//     selection doesn't do (it picks N cards independently matching a
//     per-card predicate, not "exactly N cards that share a rank"). Cutting
//     to one card per turn avoids a combination-engine build for a single
//     stress-test game (Big Two/Tichu's `climb` form needs one for a
//     structurally different reason — beating combinations of a *fixed*
//     size the leader chose — and doesn't fit here either, since Palace has
//     no "pass": a player without a legal play must pick up, not skip).
//     Four-of-a-kind burning is still modelled, via a same-rank run counter
//     across consecutive single-card turns (whoever plays the fourth
//     matching card in a row still burns the pile) — see GAP below on why
//     this had to be a scalar counter rather than reading the pile itself.
//  3. Starting player: the rules don't say who leads the first card (only
//     who leads later — the winner of the burn/no-legal-play cycles keeps
//     playing). This file starts with the dealer's left-hand neighbour, the
//     convention every clockwise game in the corpus uses for its opening
//     lead (Bridge, Pinochle: `dealer offset_by left`).
//  4. The pre-play swap is modelled as each player, in seat order, swapping
//     zero or more pairs and then declaring done, rather than every player
//     deciding simultaneously in private. See the GAP note on the `swap`
//     phase below: the `simultaneously:` block's fixed grammar (no loops,
//     no `let`, no branches in its body) can't express "swap an
//     unbounded, self-chosen number of pairs," so a later player in the
//     sequential order sees an earlier player's *finished* face-up
//     selection before choosing their own — a strictly sequential game would
//     not leak that. Face-up cards are public regardless, so hands never
//     leak; only the *order* in which swap decisions become visible differs
//     from true simultaneity.
//
// GAP: no reusable statement-level procedure. `play_from_hand`,
// `play_from_faceup`, and `play_from_blind` share ~15 lines of "resolve the
// played card against the pile" logic (rank/wild/burn bookkeeping) that the
// language has no way to factor: `function` only factors *expressions*
// (hermetic, non-recursive, no statements), and there is no move-type-body
// equivalent. The three move types below triplicate that block by hand.
//
// GAP: `action.card` is not bound inside a game-defined `move_type`'s own
// `effect` body (confirmed empirically — evaluates `action` as `none` and
// crashes on field access). It *is* bound for `transition_to: ... when
// move_type where action.card...` and rule `demands: actions where...`
// (both corpus-used), just not for a move's own effect reading what it just
// moved. Worked around below by routing every play through a one-card
// staging zone (`played`) and reading its content back via the
// `sum`/`max over <zone> as x: ...` aggregation idiom the corpus already
// uses for "the best/matching card in a collection" (Pinochle's
// `MustHeadTrick`) — a size-1 aggregate is an exact scalar read, so no
// identity ever needs extracting from a zone as a bare value.
//
// GAP: there is no `.top` / `.non_empty` / `.highest_by` zone-query method
// implemented (library.md and decisions.md describe a fuller `ZoneContents`
// API, but the type checker's method table — `cardlang/stdlib/signatures.py`
// — only wires up `.where` and `.cards_of_suit`). "The pile's effective top
// rank" therefore can't be read off the pile zone directly at all — not just
// because burying history would make `max over pile` wrong the instant a 2
// is played (which it would), but because there is no zone-level "top"
// notion to read in the first place. It has to be tracked as ordinary
// scalar state (`top_rank_value`, `top_is_wild`), written by every move that
// changes it. This is a real, load-bearing gap for any game with a
// persistent (not per-trick) discard pile with special-rank resets.
//
// GAP: `legal_moves:` is checked against a closed, hardcoded stdlib name
// table (`cardlang/stdlib/moves.py`), not the set of `move_type`s the game
// itself declares — a fully custom vocabulary (this file's six move types,
// none of them library names) cannot be listed there at all; the resolver
// rejects every one of them by name. Seven-Card Stud's betting moves hit
// the same wall and simply omit `legal_moves:` for those phases; this file
// follows the same precedent (no `legal_moves:` clause appears below).
//
// The three-layer per-player holding (hidden hand, public face-up, and
// blind-even-to-owner face-down) is expressed with library zone types only:
// `Hand<player>` and `PublicHand<player>` as documented, and `blind[player]`
// declared as `FaceDownPile` — a library type documented as a *singleton*
// stock zone (Schnapsen's talon), but the resolver checks a zone's `[index]`
// (whether it's a per-player family) completely separately from the zone
// *type*'s own owner-arity, so `FaceDownPile` indexed by player type-checks,
// and its owner/others projection table is symmetric (`count_only` both
// ways) — exactly "not even the owner knows," with no new zone type needed.
// See the `swap`/`play` phases for how blind cards are then *played*
// without ever letting the owner peek: the reveal is a random `move`, not a
// player choice (see the `play_from_blind` move type below).

game Palace {

  players: 4
  direction: clockwise

  cards: standard52
  // 2 and 10 are wild/special (never compared by rank — see `legal_play`
  // below); their position here is otherwise arbitrary.
  ranking: 3 4 5 6 7 8 9 J Q K A 10 2

  zones {
    deck           : Deck             // the stock
    hand[player]   : Hand<player>     // private hand
    faceup[player] : PublicHand<player>   // public table cards, owner included
    blind[player]  : FaceDownPile     // count_only to everyone, INCLUDING the owner
    played         : TrickPile        // one-card staging zone for the card just played
    pile           : TrickPile        // the shared, accumulating discard pile
    burn           : Burn             // cards removed from play for good
  }

  state {
    dealer         : Player  = 0
    current        : Player  = 0      // whose turn it is
    top_rank_value : Integer = 0 - 1  // -1 = pile is "empty" (anything goes)
    top_is_wild    : Boolean = false  // true when a 2 is the effective top
    run_rank_value : Integer = 0 - 1  // rank_value of the run currently on top
    run_length     : Integer = 0      // consecutive same-rank plays on top
    again          : Boolean = false  // does the actor who just played go again?
    done_swap[player] : Boolean = false
  }

  phase setup {
    shuffle deck
    deal 3 cards from deck to each blind
    deal 3 cards from deck to each faceup
    deal 3 cards from deck to each hand
    current := dealer offset_by left
  }

  // Each player, in seat order, may swap any number of hand/face-up pairs
  // before play starts (see the header GAP note on why this is sequential,
  // not simultaneous).
  phase swap {
    for each player p:
      repeat until done_swap[p] {
        offer to p one of [swap_one, stop_swapping]
      }
  }

  phase play {
    repeat until (number of players where has_cards(player)) <= 1 {
      offer to current one of
        [play_from_hand, play_from_faceup, play_from_blind, pick_up_pile]
      if has_cards(current) {
        if again {
          // the actor plays again: `current` is unchanged
        } else {
          current := next_active(current)
        }
      } else {
        current := next_active(current)
      }
    }
  }

  loser: the player where has_cards(player)
}

// === Helpers ===

function has_cards(p : Player) =
  hand[p] is not empty or faceup[p] is not empty or blind[p] is not empty

// Players fixed at 4, so at most 3 hops around the ring find the next
// player still holding cards.
function next_active(p : Player) =
  if has_cards(p offset_by left) then p offset_by left
  elif has_cards(p offset_by left offset_by left) then p offset_by left offset_by left
  elif has_cards(p offset_by left offset_by left offset_by left)
    then p offset_by left offset_by left offset_by left
  else p

// A card beats (or ties) the pile's effective top if it's a wild 2, an
// always-legal 10, the pile is "empty" (nothing to beat), the effective top
// is itself a 2 (wild — anything goes), or its rank is >= the top's.
function legal_by_values(rank_val : Integer, is_two : Boolean, is_ten : Boolean) =
  is_two or is_ten or top_rank_value < 0 or top_is_wild or rank_val >= top_rank_value

function legal_play(c : Card) = legal_by_values(rank_value(c), c.rank == "2", c.rank == "10")

// === Pre-play swap ===

move_type swap_one {
  when: hand[actor] is not empty and faceup[actor] is not empty
  effect {
    transfer chosen 1 card from hand[actor] to faceup[actor]
    transfer chosen 1 card from faceup[actor] to hand[actor]
  }
}

move_type stop_swapping {
  effect { done_swap[actor] := true }
}

// === Play ===

move_type play_from_hand {
  when: hand[actor] is not empty and
        (sum over hand[actor] as c: if legal_play(c) then 1 else 0) > 0
  effect {
    transfer chosen 1 card from hand[actor] where c => legal_play(c) to played
    let new_rank = max over played as c: rank_value(c)
    let is_two   = (sum over played as c: if c.rank == "2" then 1 else 0) > 0
    let is_ten   = (sum over played as c: if c.rank == "10" then 1 else 0) > 0
    move all cards from played to pile
    if new_rank == run_rank_value {
      run_length := run_length + 1
    } else {
      run_length := 1
      run_rank_value := new_rank
    }
    if is_ten or run_length >= 4 {
      move all cards from pile to burn
      run_length := 0
      run_rank_value := 0 - 1
      top_rank_value := 0 - 1
      top_is_wild := false
      again := true
    } else {
      if is_two {
        top_is_wild := true
        again := false
      } else {
        top_rank_value := new_rank
        top_is_wild := false
        again := false
      }
    }
    // Replenish from stock back up to 3, stock permitting.
    let hand_size = count over hand[actor] as c: true
    let short = 3 - hand_size
    if short > 0 {
      let stock = count over deck as c: true
      let draw_amt = if short < stock then short else stock
      if draw_amt > 0 { draw draw_amt cards from deck to hand[actor] }
    }
  }
}

move_type play_from_faceup {
  when: hand[actor] is empty and faceup[actor] is not empty and
        (sum over faceup[actor] as c: if legal_play(c) then 1 else 0) > 0
  effect {
    transfer chosen 1 card from faceup[actor] where c => legal_play(c) to played
    let new_rank = max over played as c: rank_value(c)
    let is_two   = (sum over played as c: if c.rank == "2" then 1 else 0) > 0
    let is_ten   = (sum over played as c: if c.rank == "10" then 1 else 0) > 0
    move all cards from played to pile
    if new_rank == run_rank_value {
      run_length := run_length + 1
    } else {
      run_length := 1
      run_rank_value := new_rank
    }
    if is_ten or run_length >= 4 {
      move all cards from pile to burn
      run_length := 0
      run_rank_value := 0 - 1
      top_rank_value := 0 - 1
      top_is_wild := false
      again := true
    } else {
      if is_two {
        top_is_wild := true
        again := false
      } else {
        top_rank_value := new_rank
        top_is_wild := false
        again := false
      }
    }
  }
}

// Blind cards are unseen even by their owner (`blind[player]` is
// `count_only` to everyone, owner included), so there is nothing to filter a
// legal candidate from: the flip is a chance event (`random`, not `chosen`),
// and legality is discovered only after the card lands face-up in `played`.
// If it doesn't beat the pile, the whole pile — including the flipped card —
// goes to the actor's hand, exactly as the reference rules say.
move_type play_from_blind {
  when: hand[actor] is empty and faceup[actor] is empty and blind[actor] is not empty
  effect {
    move random 1 card from blind[actor] to played
    let new_rank = max over played as c: rank_value(c)
    let is_two   = (sum over played as c: if c.rank == "2" then 1 else 0) > 0
    let is_ten   = (sum over played as c: if c.rank == "10" then 1 else 0) > 0
    if legal_by_values(new_rank, is_two, is_ten) {
      move all cards from played to pile
      if new_rank == run_rank_value {
        run_length := run_length + 1
      } else {
        run_length := 1
        run_rank_value := new_rank
      }
      if is_ten or run_length >= 4 {
        move all cards from pile to burn
        run_length := 0
        run_rank_value := 0 - 1
        top_rank_value := 0 - 1
        top_is_wild := false
        again := true
      } else {
        if is_two {
          top_is_wild := true
          again := false
        } else {
          top_rank_value := new_rank
          top_is_wild := false
          again := false
        }
      }
    } else {
      move all cards from played to hand[actor]
      move all cards from pile to hand[actor]
      run_length := 0
      run_rank_value := 0 - 1
      top_rank_value := 0 - 1
      top_is_wild := false
      again := false
    }
  }
}

// Forced pickup: legal exactly when the actor's active zone (hand, or
// face-up once hand is empty) holds no card that beats the pile. Blind play
// never reaches this move — an unplayable blind flip resolves its own
// pickup inline, above, since it isn't a choice.
move_type pick_up_pile {
  when: (hand[actor] is not empty and
         (sum over hand[actor] as c: if legal_play(c) then 1 else 0) == 0)
     or (hand[actor] is empty and faceup[actor] is not empty and
         (sum over faceup[actor] as c: if legal_play(c) then 1 else 0) == 0)
  effect {
    move all cards from pile to hand[actor]
    run_length := 0
    run_rank_value := 0 - 1
    top_rank_value := 0 - 1
    top_is_wild := false
    again := false
  }
}
